A||||19||Cliff Pennock||As simple as a bad spark-plug cable?||||1||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||08-12-2000||01:44 PM||||After more than a year with no problems whatsoever with my Fiero, I was stuck at the side of the road today with an engine that suddenly cut out and wouldn't start anymore. After about an hour or so, it started again but as soon as I revved it, it died. I noticed the sound of sparks coming from the distributor-cap - or that area. The kind people of Fiero Innovation here in Holland were kind enough of bringing me a new distributor-cap (that was a one hour drive for them, thanks Bjorn!) and when we replaced that, the problems seemed to have disappeared. The car had been standing still for over an hour now. So I drove the car back home and sure enough, after about 30min or so the engine cut out on me again. I was able to make it to my office where I have a garage and again it idled fine, just as soon as I revved it I heared those spark-sounds again and the engine cut out.<P>I don't think it's the ingnition coil or module so I was wondering if it could be something as simple as a bad sparkplug-wire? These wires are three years old and it might be time to replace them... Unfortunately, carpart stores are not opened on saturday here, so I wasn't able to replace them yet.<P>Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||08-12-2000||01:53 PM||||Humm Cliff... Sounds like a control module to me. I would also put new wires on it as well. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||08-12-2000||02:26 PM||||My bet is on the plug wires.  Reasons: if you hear sparking at the distributor, you've got spark.  If the car idles okay, but quits when you load it, it tends to point to problems with secondary ignition components (cap, rotor, plug wires, coil).<P>For now, you can try the ohmmeter checks outlined in the manuals to verify condition of the coil.  Once I repaired a Fiero that produced a healthy-looking spark when I plugged the coil wire into a spare plug (yes - <B>spare</B> - no typo), but wouldn't show spark at the ends of the plug wires.  So the spark was being generated, but apparently it didn't have enough oomph to jump the rotor-to-distributor-cap gap.  The ohmmeter checks revealed an open in the coil 2nd-ary.  Replaced coil - engine ran - happy customer!<P>What else?  Ohmmeter check the plug wires - you can do this whether or not stores are closed - and you might find the problem.  Here's hoping you'll find something obvious -like a big gash in the insulation somewhere, which you can temporarily repair with rubber hose / electrical tape, until you replace the plug wires!  Reminds me also - one time I temporarily repaired an open plug wire by pulling the terminal off the end, and sticking a paper clip down into the center of the wire.  Shaped the exposed part of the clip so it would hook onto the tip of the plug - worked until I could replace the wire.            <P>For what it's worth, whenever I change plug wires, I keep the old ones around.  Why? Because I figure that when I replace them, at least four out of the six are still good.  They sometimes come in real handy when parts stores are closed, or if the car that quits is your only means of transportation at the time.  In any case, good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !    ||||reg||14
Z||000003||Phil||08-12-2000||06:11 PM||||You could check your wires by misting some water on them while the car is running and look for a light show||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-12-2000||07:40 PM||||did you also replace the rotor with the cap? if not....||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||08-12-2000||10:45 PM||||Hi Cliff, Here is a test you can use to narrow down the problem. Get a spay bottle and put soapy water in it. Next with the car idling spray the spark plug boots first. If the car does not stall than spray the ignition cap and wire boots next. If the car is still idling try the coil and coil wire. If the part you spray causes the car to stall than you have found the ignition leak and therefore the problem.<P>The reason your car is stalling when you rev your motor is because you may have an ignition leak (sparks jumping out of the wires or coil). The resistance of the ignition system increases when you rev the motor. If the resistance to jump the spark plug is greater than jumping through a wire to a ground source than the spark energy will find another path to ground like going through a wire. The soapy water makes it easier for the ignition leak to find its path to ground.<P>I has been my experience that when an ignition leak is bad enough to stop the motor from running it is usually caused by a bad coil, coil high tension wire, or ignition module. All these things affect all the cylinders at the same time. If it were bad wires than what usually happens is the car looses just one or two cylinders and the car bucks and backfires. When you replace the cap and rotor you lowered the resistance of the ignition system, which bought you the time you needed to get home. As the motor warms up the ignition systems resistance also goes up.<P>If I were to take a guess I would say that the coil has the leak. Hope this helps you.<P>Good luck. Cliff,<P>John<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||08-13-2000||04:02 AM||||Ogre - Yes, the rotor was also replaced with the cap.<P>88Formula - The Ignition Module is only about a year old. The coil is I think still original. I'll try your suggestion with the waterspray.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-13-2000||01:49 PM||||A bad pickup could generate false data and trigger additional sparks. Rare but posible. A bad module, maybe but usually you get no spark when these quit.<P>just for giggles pull off the tach filter.... that can do weird things. Usually it just kills the spark but it could also generate spark depending on just how it's shorting out. check over the wiring for the filter as well.<P>also check over the coil and coil wire. A single wire bad usually won't kill a motor. Make it run like hell but won't kill it. The coil or it's wire tho could kill it and make the spark noises. Coils are great for getting hot and crapping out, especially the type GM uses on these motors.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||08-13-2000||05:28 PM||||Ogre, I had checked the tachfilter already (by disconnecting it) and that made no difference. So that leaves me with one of three possibilities:<P>1) Ignition Coil - I honestly wouldn't know why that would suddenly fail. But it's a possibility<P>2) Ignition Wire - Highly suspect since it's three years old. However, I have cleaned that wire and couldn't find any cracks.<P>3) Ignition Module - I most certainly hope not since it's only one year old. Granted, they're cheap, but if it has failed on me again it's certainly a fragile part.<P>It's pretty darn irritating that the problem only happens after I have driven it for 30 minutes or so, so it's not a simple replace-'n-try job. And driving circles for 30 minutes around the office (the only place where I have a garage) is not something on my wishlist. Are there any simple test (besides the waterspray) that I can try?||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||08-13-2000||08:20 PM||||Hi Cliff, to answer your question about a simple test you can do, here is one you can try.<P> Your problem seems to be heat related. Get a hair dryer or heat gun. Next with your fiero running apply heat to the coil being careful not to overheat the coil. Next try the ignition module and see what that does. The heat gun simulates the heat generated by the inherent operation of the components over that thirty minutes very quickly.<P>You can do this to the main computer under your console also because sometimes the computer develops a crack in the circuit board. The crack in the circuit board opens up when the computer heats up over time and causes an open circuit. When the computer cools down the crack closes and the circuit closes making the car run again.<P>If you say your wires are three years old then I would replace them, especially if they’re not GM wires. It has been my experience that after market wires never last very long (most are junk). If you can get GM wires than do so because they last a long time and are made specifically for your fiero.<P>Just because your ignition module is only a year old does not mean that you can rule it out especially if you have an after market module. You have to assume that it could be anything and through the process of elimination track down the problem to its source. <P>I also want to say that leaking wires don’t have to have a crack in then to leak and they can pass a ohm meter test also. The leak usually occurs right were the wire enters the boot. The best test is the soapy water test. I forgot to mention that if you can have an assistant apply the brake and load the engine while you perform the soapy water test than do it. This rises the resistance on the ignition system making it more likely for the leak to occur when you spray the defective part.<P>If you need any more help Cliff than just e-mail me and I will try to think up some more test you can try. <P>Good luck.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 08-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||86SE||08-14-2000||02:45 AM||||Check your tire pressure.||||reg||3
Z||000011||86SE||08-14-2000||02:45 AM||||Check your tire pressure.||||reg||3
Z||000012||86SE||08-14-2000||02:48 AM||||Check your tire pressure.<BR>Actually I don't have much of a clue.  Just run the car at night and pop the trunk and see where the sparks are leaking at.  Then put your hand over the spot where they are leaking to stop them from coming out.  It dosent hurt that bad.  Have you ever stuck a nife in a toaster? Me neither, but I bet it hurts worse than the spark from an engine.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||3
Z||000013||86SE||08-14-2000||02:54 AM||||Oops, tripple post.<P>I even spelled knife wrong and spelled triple wrong......im goin to bed<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>**Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention**||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||08-14-2000||03:05 PM||||I forgot about the ECM..... Those do run hot in these cars. Stuffing them under the console wasn't such a good place, I'd bet my ECM is running over 100 degrees inside most of the time. A cracked connector could easily cause BS like this.<P>The type of coil GM used on these is actually prone to this kind of problem. These coils aren't as good at dealing with heat as oilbathed ones.||||reg||1
Z||000015||triker||08-15-2000||02:19 PM||||Cliff, plug wires usually leak worse under load.  I check mine by having someone help me in a dark garage.  Have them hold the brake and with the car in gear (auto), or slip the clutch (man) and load the engine.  In the dark, any leaks show up real well.  I use a flashlight to see exactly where the leak is.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Cliff Pennock||08-16-2000||07:22 AM||||*** UPDATE ***<P>My suspicion was leaning more and more towards the ingnition coil so I thought I'd go ahead and replace it. First I wanted to recreate the problem with the old coil and ofcourse I wasn't able to. The engine didn't die on me even after the engine's temp rose to about 215F. It did hesitate a bit if I temporarily throttled full and I could hear a little of the earlier described sparkling sounds. Another weird thing was that whenever I applied full throttle, my Digital Dash would go haywire and sometimes even reset itself. This isn't all that strange because if there are false sparks somewhere, you get tremendous voltage spikes coming from ground and the Digital Dash would protect itself by shutting down its power supplies (if the spike is high enough).<P>But again, I wasn't really able to recreate the problem (I didn't want to drive it away from my garage). I decided to replace the ignition coil anyway, and see if I could notice any difference - and I did. It didn't hesitate anymore and the Dash didn't do any strange things under throttle. This is a good sign and I really hope I've tackeled the problem. I will go ahead and start driving the car again. If this was not the problem I'll notice soon enough.<P>But it does sound logical that this <B>was</B> the problem, doesn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Phil||08-16-2000||08:26 AM||||Logic has nothing to do with a Fiero, if it did they would still be making them.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Xtreme Fiero's||08-16-2000||09:59 AM||||my 84 se is doing the exact same thing, I don't have a digital dash but when I floor it the car dies down and the tach jumps all around, my fuel gauge is also stuck way past full and my volt meter says 8 volts but I know it's wrong, The car starts just fine w/ all the lights on and the volt meter barley moves at all. but my temp and speedo seem to be fine also my center lights (turn signal/oil) don't work . Is all of the related to Spark jumping from a wire/plug to ground? ||||reg||1
Z||000019||88formula||08-16-2000||05:55 PM||||Didn't I say it was the coil!||||reg||1
